A||N||13||Xtreme Fiero's||3.4?'s||||5||
Z||000000||Xtreme Fiero's||12-17-2000||09:27 PM||||<BR>I will soon be buying another 85GT in need of a engine I have a 5-speed waiting for it here. And was thinking of a Camaro 3.4L 6cyl I know about driling the starter bolts and swaping the heads & intake but Is that it? seems to simple. what abot injectors to the 2.8L injectors flow enough? can you use the 3.4 injectors? is the stock intake manifolds fole enough air for the larger displacment?<P>I have also noticed that a few peopple on the forum seem to stick with moded 2.8's (Hypnotise, Shaun#### & 1fst2m6) all seem to use a "3.2 W/ NOS" or a big honk'n turbo on thier 2.8  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Why? if a 3.4L install is so simple. <P>Thanks,<BR>   Tom<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Xtreme Fiero's (edited 12-18-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Raydar||12-17-2000||10:55 PM||||You don't have to change the heads on the Camaro 3.4. They are identical to the Fiero's. Your Fiero intake will bolt right up. Ed Parks recommends using the 3.4 injectors if you've got 'em. 5.0 injectors will work, too.<BR>Here's his 3.4 page... <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#34" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#34</A> <P>Raydar||||reg||1
Z||000002||Xtreme Fiero's||12-18-2000||01:08 AM||||Well.......I guess the 3.4 isn't as easy as I thought. Who as done this install? Is it worth all the labor? are there any FWD 3.4's that I can just swap the top end and bolt into my car?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||12-18-2000||01:33 AM||||I have done the Camaro swap, and I like the results! It is time consuming to swap everything over from one engine to the other, just take your time. There are drill guides somewhere where you can get the holes for the starter in the correct place. I used the starter as a guide and drilled/tapped the holes myself. Once again, just take your time. The FWD 3.4's use AL heads and the pistons are different from those with iron heads, so you could use the FWD block, but would need to swap in the RWD pistons and heads. Take your pick- drilling a couple of holes or swapping in all the internals. Either way, it is a good engine.||||reg||1
Z||000004||tgowens||12-18-2000||11:39 AM||||we do the 3.4 RWD swaps here at the Fiero Shop. we use Lucas 305 injectors and an adjustable fuel pressure regulator in place of the 2.8 injectors. Everything else comes from the 2.8 from the heads up. Leave the 3.4 heads on the 3.4 block. Stock 2.8 manifolds, stock intakes, etc all work on the 3.4 but, make sure you have a wiring harness from a 2.8 V-6 5-speed along with the ECM.........everything else is fairly simple. We do modify the u-shaped bracket for the front motor strut mount though, weld a stud to it for the a/c mount if you have a/c.........other than that, it is a very simple install.........the 3.4 FWD is far more complicated than the 3.4 RWD version..........||||reg||1
Z||000005||Xtreme Fiero's||12-18-2000||12:12 PM||||<P>TGOWENS: I do not plan to have A/C so is the Starter Holes the onlu thing have to do? also the 3.4L would be going into a 85 GT wich is originaly a 4-speed BUT i may be installing a Isizu 5-speed. will I need a new ECM for that? what if I keep it a 4-speed. Is there a install kit for the 3.4?||||reg||5
Z||000006||Cooter||12-18-2000||12:53 PM||||Almost forgot- You need a neutral balance flywheel from an 88 up v6.||||reg||1
Z||000007||mshill||12-18-2000||01:32 PM||||Xtreme,  I am just finishing up my 95 Camaro 3.4L engine.  If you can find someone on the forum here that will let you borrow or rent the drill guide, drill and tap, the drilling/tapping of the starter holes only take about an hour, the first time.  I am sure that it is much faster the second time.  I got a long block, but decided to have the head work done anyway just to make sure.  It has gone well and no major problems have come up.  I used my 86 timing cover and oil pan.  I purchased an 88 flywheel from someone on the forum for $65 and had it resurfaced for $15.  I have also had Darrell Morse bore out my throttle body and plenum to 57mm, underdrive pulley and roller rockers.  I am not yet purchasing larger injectors or headers because of budgeting issues. I have also herd that the 2.8 injectors are sufficient.<P>If your 2.8L is in need of a major overhaul the 3.4L is probably a really good bang for the buck.||||reg||1
Z||000008||HYPTNOTiSE||12-18-2000||01:36 PM||||Hey fiero xtreme, I live right up the road from you and i have a Starter hole drilling service so dont let that be of any concern to you brother.  It's not like you'll have to take the engine very far. A 10-15 minute ride and you can get your starter holes drilled.  It's a very easy swap.  Also if you need help finding a 3.4, i can do that for you as well.  I did Jeff with the black88gt's 3.4.  <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/MikeDrillingS_3.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>       Mike LeCompte<BR>86 Fiero GT 3.2L w/ Nitrous<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.tcac.net/~daddyg/hypsig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Xtreme Fiero's||12-18-2000||02:22 PM||||Ok I guess all that I need to know is other than the Starter holes, Can I just install the Fiero Intake & sensors and be ready to go? Are the engine mounts, Dog bone remain stock.<P>HYPNOTiSE: you got mail||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||12-18-2000||03:16 PM||||There is one FWD 3.4 engine that doesn't have the aluminum heads, that's the 3.4 with cast iron heads found in many of the early 90's GM minivans. I don't think it requires the starter being relocated, but I have been wrong before. Ed Park's website tells about this swap better than I can.||||reg||1
Z||000011||88formula||12-18-2000||05:17 PM||||There is a small check valve in the oil passage right above the oil filter. You can see it when you pull the oil filter off of your old 2.8-liter engine. If the 3.4-liter engine does not have this check valve than you have to remove it from your 2.8-liter engine and install it into your 3.4-liter engine. One of the three 3.4-liter engines I got did not have this valve. It is an anti-drainback valve. Also all the engines I got were supposed to have between 50-60 thousand miles on them but when I pulled the heads off of them they all had rust in the cylinders or severe wear and sludge buildup. I decided after that to just buy a core and rebuild the engine the way I wanted it. Watch out some junk yard owners are lying crooks!<P>Just bolting a 3.4-liter engine with ported exhaust manifolds and no other modifications will yield you 165 horsepower @ 5000 rpm and 207-ft. lbs. of torque @ 3000 rpm. With the five speed transmission and a 2925 lb. weight your fiero will go 0-60 in 6.65 seconds and the quarter mile in 14.8 seconds @ 91 mph compared to 8 seconds and 16 for the stock 2.8.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Hawk||12-18-2000||05:59 PM||||Keep in mind that you can neutral balance your 2.8 flywheel, it cast me $50 at my balancing shop.<P>87 Fiero GT<BR>soon to be 3.4 with 5speed trans.||||reg||1
